00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:58.360
All right, let me start with a video&nbsp;
entitled "Fewer Than One in 5,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:05.566
Meet Sodium and Potassium Recommended&nbsp;
Intakes". Play video one. Let’s do it.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:29.840
Worldwide, physical inactivity accounted
for more than 10 million years

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:36.360
of healthy life lost, but what we eat
accounts for nearly 20 times that.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:40.680
Unhealthy diets shave hundreds of
millions of disability-free years off

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:43.240
of people's lives every year.

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:47.600
What are the worst aspects of our diets?
Four out of the five of the deadliest

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:51.280
dietary traps involve not eating
enough of certain foods.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:53.960
Not eating enough whole grains,
not eating enough fruits,

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:57.200
not eating enough nuts and seeds,
and not eating enough vegetables.

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:01.560
But our most fatal flaw
is too much salt.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:05.320
That's on the order of 15 times
deadlier than diets too high in soda,

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:07.920
for example, just to keep
things in perspective.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:10.920
There remains no single more
effective public health action

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:16.320
related to nutrition than the
reduction of sodium in the diet.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:19.200
This is why national and international
health organizations have called

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.520
for warning labels on salt
packets and salt shakers,

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:26.720
with messages like "Too much sodium
in the diet causes high blood pressure

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:29.320
and increases risk of stomach
cancer, stroke, heart disease,

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:33.200
and kidney disease.
Limit your use."

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:36.800
Salt also increases inflammation.
For example, sodium intake

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:40.560
is associated with increased disease
activity in multiple sclerosis,

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.080
an inflammatory autoimmune
nerve condition,

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:46.000
about three to four times
the exacerbation rate in those

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:47.760
with medium or high sodium intakes

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:52.560
compared to those getting less than
a teaspoon of salt total in a day.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:55.480
Just as you can see higher sodium
levels in the tissues of those

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:59.760
who suffer from lupus, another serious
inflammatory autoimmune disease,

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:03.640
you can correlate high sodium levels
in the spinal cord of MS patients

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:06.880
with the disease and decreased
structural integrity.

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:09.840
Where's sodium found though?
Really crappy foods.

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:12.200
So it's hard to know
if increased salt intake

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:15.040
is just a marker for
a bad diet overall.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:17.680
But what we do know is that
salt and high blood pressure

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:19.680
are cause-and-effect.
How?

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:22.760
Because we have more than a
hundred randomized controlled trials

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:26.600
demonstrating that if you cut down on
added salt, your blood pressures fall,

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:31.320
and the more you cut down, the better.
Part of the mechanism may actually be

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:34.840
the damage salt may do to your
microbiome, the friendly flora

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:38.080
in your gut.
And no wonder.

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:42.240
Our bodies evolved only to
handle about 750 mg a day.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:44.680
The American Heart Association
says we should stay under

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:47.240
at least twice that about,

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:50.960
but we're eating more than
four times what's natural.

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:55.200
And it's only getting worse,
increasing over the last decade.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:57.840
Anyone care to guess what
percentage of Americans exceed

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:01.200
the 1,500 mg upper
limit recommendation?

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:05.920
98.8%. And that was
more than a decade ago.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:10.080
The vast majority of U.S. adults
consume too much sodium, and

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:14.680
at the same time, too little potassium,
a mineral that lowers blood pressure.

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:17.240
Less than 2% of U.S. adults
consumed the recommended

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:19.680
daily minimum intake of potassium.

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:24.560
So more than 98% of Americans
eat potassium deficient diets.

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:27.960
This deficiency is even more striking
when comparing our current intake

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:32.280
with that of our ancestors, who consumed
large amounts of dietary potassium.

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:36.240
We evolved getting probably
more than 10,000 mg a day.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:39.280
The recommendation is to get
around at least around half that,

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:43.200
yet most of us don't even
come anywhere close.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:46.360
Put the two guidelines together,
and sodium and potassium goals

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:51.640
are currently met by less than
0.015% of the U.S. population.

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:55.880
So we're talking close
to 99.99% noncompliance,

00:06:55.880 --> 00:07:01.240
as in only 1 in 6,000 Americans even
hits the recommended guidelines.

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:05.200
What about using potassium-based
salt substitutes?

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:07.680
Instead of using
sodium chloride—salt—

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:10.640
why not shake on
some potassium chloride?

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:14.080
Seems a little too good to be true.
I mean, same salty taste, but you're

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:18.080
reducing sodium while at the same time
increasing your potassium intake?

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:20.160
Is there a catch?

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:24.200
Are potassium-based salt
substitutes safe? Effective?

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:29.520
We'll find out next.

00:07:31.633 --> 00:08:24.833
(Dr. Greger introduces next video)

00:08:42.040 --> 00:08:46.240
Of all the terrible things about
our diets, high dietary sodium intake,

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:50.040
high salt intake, is the leading risk,
estimated to be causing millions

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:54.280
of deaths every year, killing millions
mainly through adverse effects on

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:56.680
blood pressure and increased
risk of stroke, heart attack, and

00:08:56.680 --> 00:08:59.240
kidney damage. Hypertension—
high blood pressure—

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:02.440
is still called the
"silent and invisible killer"

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:05.720
because it rarely causes symptoms,
but is one of the most powerful

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:10.400
independent predictors of some
of our leading causes of death.

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:13.880
We evolved consuming a diet very
rich in potassium and low in sodium,

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:16.800
but today this pattern has
been completely reversed.

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:19.960
This flip reflects a shift from
traditional plant-based diets

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:22.040
high in potassium
and low in sodium,

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:24.720
a shift from fruits, greens,
roots, and tubers

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:28.280
to salty processed foods stripped
of potassium, which is considered

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:32.640
a nutrient of public health concern
because 98% of the U.S. population

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:35.920
doesn't even reach the recommended
minimum daily intake of potassium,

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:38.360
as I mentioned in the last video.

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:41.920
And low potassium intake itself is
implicated in high blood pressure

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:45.600
and cardiovascular disease, yet
few physicians actually think about

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:48.120
proposing their patients eat more
foods that are potassium rich,

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:50.640
like fruits and vegetables, to
better control blood pressure,

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:53.560
even though several meta-analyses
have now confirmed

00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:58.640
that high potassium intake appears
to reduce the risk of stroke.

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:02.800
There's even a reduction in stroke risk
independent of blood pressure effects,

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:05.200
consistent with other protective
effects of potassium,

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:08.920
such as reducing clot formation,
reducing hardening of the arteries,

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:11.800
reducing the generation
of free radicals.

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:14.960
Higher sodium intake is associated
with a 20% increased risk

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:18.320
of dying prematurely,
where higher potassium intake

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:22.680
was associated with a 20%
reduced risk of dying prematurely.

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:25.720
Yeah, but sodium is found
in crappy processed foods,

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:29.160
while potassium is concentrated in
healthy foods like beans and greens.

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:33.600
So having low sodium intake and high
potassium intake may just be a marker

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:37.400
for a healthful diet: more
plant foods and less crap.

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:40.320
How do we know sodium
is cause-and-effect bad?

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:43.320
Because randomized controlled trials
show sodium reduction leads

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:46.600
to blood pressure reduction,
just like there are randomized

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:49.560
controlled trials showing that if
you give people extra potassium,

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:52.440
you can bring down their
blood pressures as well.

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:56.320
So what about using potassium
chloride to salt your food,

00:10:56.320 --> 00:10:58.840
rather than sodium chloride?

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:02.480
That's what's found in these
zero sodium salt substitutes.

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:05.240
Potassium chloride is a
naturally occurring mineral salt,

00:11:05.240 --> 00:11:08.480
which is obtained the same way
we get regular sodium salt.

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:11.360
Since we get too much sodium
and not enough potassium,

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:16.320
this would seem to make potassium
chloride a win-win solution.

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:18.960
Now, whole healthy plant foods
would be the best way

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:20.840
to increase potassium intake.

00:11:20.840 --> 00:11:23.960
Fruits and vegetables have all sorts
of other good things in them besides

00:11:23.960 --> 00:11:27.880
potassium, but we have 10 studies
now, randomized controlled trials,

00:11:27.880 --> 00:11:31.480
showing that just swapping in some
potassium chloride for regular salt

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:34.200
can lead to significant reductions
in blood pressure in people

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:37.480
with hypertension, suggesting
that salt substitutes may even help

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:39.600
prevent hypertension as well.

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:42.440
We know salt substitutes
can lower blood pressure,

00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:45.720
but does it actually decrease
the incidence of hypertension,

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:48.840
and more importantly, disease
endpoints like stroke and mortality?

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:52.880
You don't know until
you put it to the test.

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:56.560
In a randomized controlled trial,
households had their salt replaced

00:11:56.560 --> 00:12:00.400
with just a quarter potassium chloride.
At that level, most people either

00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:05.080
can't tell the difference or even prefer
the salt with the potassium mixed in.

00:12:05.080 --> 00:12:07.600
Okay, but did it actually do any good?

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:11.000
The use of even the quarter
salt substitute was associated

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:15.480
with cutting the risk of
developing hypertension in half.

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:18.920
Okay, but what about actually following
people out to see if there's any change

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:22.880
in the risk of dying from
cardiovascular disease?

00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:27.720
Five kitchens in a veterans' retirement
home were randomized into two groups

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:31.360
for about two and a half years,
salting meals with regular salt

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:33.920
or, unbeknownst to the
cooks and the diners,

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:38.120
a 50/50 blend of potassium chloride.

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:42.040
Those in the half potassium group cut
their risk of dying from cardiovascular

00:12:42.040 --> 00:12:46.200
disease by about 40% and lived
up to nearly a year longer.

00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:50.160
The life expectancy difference
at age 70 was equivalent to that

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:52.240
which would have naturally
occurred in 14 years,

00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:55.360
meaning that just switching
to half potassium salt appeared

00:12:55.360 --> 00:12:58.440
to effectively make people more
than a decade younger

00:12:58.440 --> 00:13:01.000
when it came to risk of death.

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:03.960
As we speak, there's a massive
randomized controlled trial wrapping up

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:07.840
in China involving 600 villages and
more than 20,000 people that will

00:13:07.840 --> 00:13:12.600
give us an idea of just how low we
can drop stroke risk with this strategy.

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:16.520
China is perfect because up to 75%
of their sodium intake comes from

00:13:16.520 --> 00:13:19.400
salt they add in the home
kitchen or dining room,

00:13:19.400 --> 00:13:22.240
whereas most sodium in the
American diet comes

00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:25.520
prepackaged in the meat
and processed foods we buy,

00:13:25.520 --> 00:13:28.800
though certainly the food companies
could switch over themselves.

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:31.680
Why haven't they? And why
haven't more people embraced

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:36.440
these salt substitutes if they work
well and can taste just as good?

00:13:36.440 --> 00:13:38.280
We could be "Achieving the Benefits

00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:41.720
of a High-Potassium Paleolithic
Diet, Without the Toxicity."

00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:46.000
So "Is salt substitution ready
for prime time" or what?

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:47.280
What about safety?

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:49.480
Yes, there are convincing
arguments about the benefits,

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:51.880
but what about the
risks, like, you know,

00:13:51.880 --> 00:13:57.840
the "inclusion of potentially fatal
salt substitutes in the food supply."

00:13:57.840 --> 00:13:59.200
Wait, what?

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:03.642
We'll talk about the
potential downsides next.

00:14:05.996 --> 00:15:21.475
(Dr. Greger recaps and introduces next video)

00:15:31.360 --> 00:15:35.000
Based on how we evolved, the
Optimum Dietary Potassium Intake

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:39.280
likely greatly exceeds current
and even recommended intakes.

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:42.280
The problem is we replaced many
of the potassium-rich plant foods

00:15:42.280 --> 00:15:45.160
we used to stuff our faces with,
like fruits, leafy greens,

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:47.600
and other vegetables,
including roots and tubers,

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:50.400
with calorie-dense junk---
stripped not only of fiber

00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:54.320
but also potassium---such
as added fats and sugars.

00:15:54.320 --> 00:15:56.880
So in a traditional,
largely plant-based diet,

00:15:56.880 --> 00:16:00.120
potassium content is high
and sodium content is low.

00:16:00.120 --> 00:16:03.040
But now, high blood pressure's
the second leading cause of death

00:16:03.040 --> 00:16:05.680
in the world, killing more than
10 million people a year,

00:16:05.680 --> 00:16:10.520
second only to unhealthy diets,
the #1 killer of humanity.

00:16:10.520 --> 00:16:13.800
We can improve both by eating more
whole healthy plant foods like beans

00:16:13.800 --> 00:16:18.160
and greens, which are packed with
potassium, which lowers blood pressure.

00:16:18.160 --> 00:16:21.200
But since most of us are getting
too much sodium along with

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:24.200
too little potassium, what about
using salt substitutes?

00:16:24.200 --> 00:16:27.600
After all, the most commonly used
salt substitute is potassium chloride,

00:16:27.600 --> 00:16:31.600
so you'd be swapping out
sodium for potassium.

00:16:31.600 --> 00:16:32.560
And it works.

00:16:32.560 --> 00:16:35.400
Based on a meta-analysis of
more than a dozen randomized

00:16:35.400 --> 00:16:38.600
controlled trials, replacing sodium
chloride with potassium chloride

00:16:38.600 --> 00:16:40.000
lowers blood pressure.

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:42.400
And most of the trials involved
just swapping out regular salt

00:16:42.400 --> 00:16:46.760
for less than 30% potassium
chloride, and they still got results.

00:16:46.760 --> 00:16:49.760
And at less than 30%, most people
can't even tell the difference

00:16:49.760 --> 00:16:52.640
between regular salt
and the potassium salt.

00:16:52.640 --> 00:16:56.600
So it can taste exactly the same
and drop your blood pressures?

00:16:56.600 --> 00:16:58.320
What's the catch?

00:16:58.320 --> 00:17:01.800
Potassium chloride is generally
considered as safe by the FDA,

00:17:01.800 --> 00:17:04.320
with the only major concern for
healthy people being that if you

00:17:04.320 --> 00:17:08.120
go completely 100% sodium free
and use straight potassium chloride,

00:17:08.120 --> 00:17:10.320
like all these, it can
taste kind of funny,

00:17:10.320 --> 00:17:12.560
adding a bitter or metallic taste.

00:17:12.560 --> 00:17:15.920
I've found that it depends on
what I'm seasoning with it.

00:17:15.920 --> 00:17:18.760
It works perfectly well on some
things, but makes other things

00:17:18.760 --> 00:17:20.360
completely inedible.

00:17:20.360 --> 00:17:23.200
When I learned about the sodium
science and threw out my salt shaker

00:17:23.200 --> 00:17:25.920
for good, within a few weeks
my palate totally changed,

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:29.920
and everything tasted fine
without salt—except pesto.

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:33.440
For some reason, pesto without salt
just never tasted like it used to, to me.

00:17:34.040 --> 00:17:37.880
So I tried the potassium chloride salt
substitute, and it worked perfectly;

00:17:37.880 --> 00:17:41.920
I couldn't tell the difference at all,
so I had the best of both worlds.

00:17:41.920 --> 00:17:46.240
Then I remembered how as a kid
I used to put a tiny sprinkle of salt

00:17:46.240 --> 00:17:49.880
on watermelon like they do in
the South to make it even sweeter.

00:17:49.880 --> 00:17:53.760
So I tried it with the potassium
salt and almost gagged.

00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:56.640
So it's definitely not for everything.

00:17:56.640 --> 00:17:58.480
The reason healthy people
don't have to worry about

00:17:58.480 --> 00:18:00.000
getting too much potassium

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:02.880
is that our kidneys just
pee out the excess.

00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:05.920
Okay, but that's with potassium
in food. What about supplements?

00:18:05.920 --> 00:18:08.680
No adverse effects have been
shown for long-term intakes

00:18:08.680 --> 00:18:11.880
of potassium supplements
as high as 3,000 mg a day.

00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:15.120
And in fact, blood levels of potassium
are maintained in the normal range

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:18.440
by healthy kidneys even when
potassium intake is increased

00:18:18.440 --> 00:18:24.600
to approximately 15,000 mg a day,
which is no surprise since we evolved

00:18:24.600 --> 00:18:28.560
eating so many healthy plant foods,
so many fruits and vegetables

00:18:28.560 --> 00:18:32.160
that the normal, natural potassium
intake for the human species

00:18:32.160 --> 00:18:35.720
may be on the order
of 15,000 mg a day.

00:18:35.720 --> 00:18:39.160
Basically, the normal range
for potassium levels in the blood

00:18:39.160 --> 00:18:41.320
is between 3.5 and 5.0,

00:18:41.320 --> 00:18:45.040
and you start to worry when
it starts creeping up towards 6.

00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:48.560
But give people potassium
supplements, like all the salt

00:18:48.560 --> 00:18:51.360
substitution trials that have been
giving study subjects an average

00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:53.920
of about 2,000 mg
of potassium a day,

00:18:53.920 --> 00:19:00.280
and blood levels only go up 0.14.
So they might go up from 4 to 4.14,

00:19:00.280 --> 00:19:03.680
not something that would
push you into trouble.

00:19:03.680 --> 00:19:05.000
Now there's a limit.

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:09.400
If you have a "massive banana eating
habit", you could bump your potassium

00:19:09.400 --> 00:19:13.360
from normal to above 6, but that was
evidently from years of not eating

00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:16.760
much of anything except
up to 20 bananas a day.

00:19:16.760 --> 00:19:21.560
Eating ten pounds of carrots every
day is also probably not a good idea.

00:19:21.560 --> 00:19:26.960
That's like 75 carrots in one day,
which you could only do with a juicer,

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:31.400
which is what this person attempted
to do as part of a quack cancer cure.

00:19:31.400 --> 00:19:34.720
What about overdoing salt substitutes?

00:19:34.720 --> 00:19:37.760
This report from the 1940's
was on lithium poisoning

00:19:37.760 --> 00:19:39.280
from the use of salt substitutes.

00:19:39.280 --> 00:19:44.640
Why? Because lithium chloride was
used as a salt substitute — Jesus!

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:48.440
But what about potassium chloride,
which is what's used today?

00:19:48.440 --> 00:19:52.920
There is one fatal case of someone
who committed suicide by downing

00:19:52.920 --> 00:19:57.280
a little more than a tablespoon of a
potassium chloride salt substitute.

00:19:57.280 --> 00:20:00.880
That doesn't seem like a lot,
just a tablespoon?

00:20:00.880 --> 00:20:03.040
I mean, how can we keep
that on the shelves

00:20:03.040 --> 00:20:06.040
if only a tablespoon will kill you?

00:20:06.040 --> 00:20:11.480
Well, even less than that of regular
salt taken at once can kill you, too.

00:20:11.480 --> 00:20:14.280
In fact, salt water ingestion was
evidently a traditional method

00:20:14.280 --> 00:20:17.520
of suicide in ancient China,
though these days

00:20:17.520 --> 00:20:19.400
one may be more likely
to die that way

00:20:19.400 --> 00:20:22.280
from abusive religious practices.

00:20:22.280 --> 00:20:25.240
Having said all that, there are
a small number of the population

00:20:25.240 --> 00:20:26.520
who may run into problems,

00:20:26.520 --> 00:20:31.120
primarily those with severely
impaired kidney function.

00:20:31.120 --> 00:20:33.480
That's why there's been such
a reluctance to push

00:20:33.480 --> 00:20:36.960
potassium-based salt substitutes
on a population level.

00:20:36.960 --> 00:20:39.200
If your kidneys can't regulate
your potassium,

00:20:39.200 --> 00:20:41.880
then you can definitely run
into a serious issue.

00:20:41.880 --> 00:20:45.400
We're talking about folks with
known kidney disease, diabetes---

00:20:45.400 --> 00:20:48.440
since diabetes can lead to kidney
damage---severe heart failure,

00:20:48.440 --> 00:20:51.800
those on medications that impair
potassium excretion, older adults,

00:20:51.800 --> 00:20:54.640
and individuals with
adrenal insufficiency.

00:20:54.640 --> 00:20:56.440
If you aren't sure if you're at risk,

00:20:56.440 --> 00:21:00.360
ask your doctor about getting
your kidney function tested.

00:21:00.360 --> 00:21:03.640
Ironically, potassium is so
good at reducing deaths

00:21:03.640 --> 00:21:06.800
from high blood pressure, even
among those with kidney disease,

00:21:06.800 --> 00:21:09.800
using potassium chloride salt
substitutes would probably

00:21:09.800 --> 00:21:12.840
still save more lives despite the risk.

00:21:12.840 --> 00:21:16.440
Traditional dietary recommendations
to kidney patients limited the intake

00:21:16.440 --> 00:21:19.600
of fruits and vegetables because
of their high potassium content.

00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:22.000
However, this paradigm is
rapidly changing due to

00:21:22.000 --> 00:21:25.640
the multiple benefits derived from
a fundamentally plant-based diet.

00:21:25.640 --> 00:21:29.680
A whole food plant-based diet may even
ameliorate chronic kidney disease.

00:21:29.680 --> 00:21:31.880
There's increasing evidence
that a whole food plant-based diet

00:21:31.880 --> 00:21:35.400
may offer benefits like slowing the
progression of chronic kidney disease

00:21:35.400 --> 00:21:37.760
and delaying kidney failure.

00:21:37.760 --> 00:21:40.840
So the practice of restricting dietary
potassium in kidney patients

00:21:40.840 --> 00:21:44.800
should really be reserved for patients
with documented hyperkalemia,

00:21:44.800 --> 00:21:47.480
a potassium level of 6 or higher,

00:21:47.480 --> 00:21:51.040
because the key to halting
progression of chronic kidney disease

00:21:51.040 --> 00:21:56.197
may in fact lie in the produce aisle.

00:21:58.973 --> 00:22:49.770
(Dr. Greger recaps and takes a break for Q&amp;A session)

00:22:50.374 --> 00:55:03.915
(Q&amp;A session not transcribed)

